    "q-262452": {
      "dom_id": "step-q262452",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254920/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0+0=0.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
