    "q-262532": {
      "dom_id": "step-q262532",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the value of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({270}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({270}^{∘})=\\,$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The angles ${180}^{∘}$ and ${270}^{∘}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254920/assets/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\n- Let's first consider the angle ${180}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$  Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Now, let's consider the angle ${270}^{∘}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${270}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$  Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({270}^{∘})=-1.$ Therefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡({270}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({270}^{∘})}=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡({180}^{∘})+\\operatorname{csc}⁡({270}^{∘})=-1+(-1)=-1-1=-2.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
